<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Auld Lang Syne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1788">1788</a> (set to its present tune <a href="/years/1799">1799</a>)</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Auld Lang Syne&quot; is a poem in Scots that Robert Burns wrote in 1788 on the basis of an older Scottish folk song. Burns sent it to the Scots Musical Museum that year, describing it as an old song that had never been printed or written down until he took it down from an old man.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The text is a Scots poem written by Robert Burns in 1788 on the basis of an older Scottish folk song. Burns sent it to the Scots Musical Museum with the remark that it was an old song, of the olden times, which had never been in print nor even in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> until he took it down from an old man.</p><p>Some of the words were indeed collected rather than composed. A ballad, Old Long Syne, printed by James Watson in 1711, resembles the first verse and chorus closely and almost certainly derives from the same old song, so it is a fair supposition that the rest of the poem is Burns's own. The phrase auld lang syne also occurs in poems by Robert Ayton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allan-ramsay-poet">Allan Ramsay</a> and James Watson, and in older folk songs. The Scots title may be rendered as old long since, or less literally as days gone by.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The song opens by asking whether old times should be forgotten, which is generally read as a call to remember long-standing friendships. In George Thomson's Select Songs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> of 1799 the second verse, about greeting and toasting, was moved to its present place at the end. The song originally had another melody, traced to about 1700 and judged mediocre by Burns. The first documented use of the tune now standard was in 1799, in Thomson's second volume. It is a pentatonic Scots folk melody, probably once a sprightly dance at a much quicker tempo, and there is some doubt that Burns intended it.</p><p>The composer William Shield seems to quote it in the overture to his opera Rosina of 1782, but both may have taken it from a common source, possibly a strathspey. In 1792 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-haydn">Joseph Haydn</a> arranged it for George Thomson, and Beethoven later made an arrangement too.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Singing the song on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hogmanay">Hogmanay</a>, or New Year's Eve, quickly became a Scots custom that spread to other parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>, and emigrants took it round the world. At Hogmanay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> everyone joins hands in a circle and crosses arms at the last verse, while the tradition of singing it when parting with hands crossed arose in the mid-19th century among Freemasons and other fraternal organisations. It is also used at funerals, graduations, the closing of Scout jamborees and Burns suppers.</p><p>The melody has carried other words. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the Jesuit Jacques Sevin translated the song in 1920 as Ce n'est qu'un au revoir. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> the tune serves for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hotaru-no-hikari">Hotaru no Hikari</a>, played at school graduations. It was the tune of the Korean exile government's anthem from 1919 to 1945, and of the anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a> until 1972. The first recording was made on a wax cylinder in 1898.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Auld Lang Syne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The words were first sung to the tune of the Scottish song &quot;<b>Auld Lang Syne</b>&quot;, known in Korea as &quot;The Song of Farewell&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/aegukga">Aegukga</a></li><li><blockquote>The Korean government in exile in Shanghai (1919-1945) had adopted the older &quot;Aegukga&quot;, sung to the tune of &quot;<b>Auld Lang Syne</b>&quot;, as its anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/aegukka">Aegukka (North Korea)</a></li><li><blockquote>The melody was often said to come from a traditional Scottish folk song of uncertain origin, as &quot;Hotaru no Hikari&quot; borrowed the tune of &quot;<b>Auld Lang Syne</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/aogeba-totoshi">Aogeba Tōtoshi</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Auld Lang Syne</b>&quot; is a poem in Scots that Robert Burns wrote in 1788 on the basis of an older Scottish folk song.</blockquote><a href="/songs/auld-lang-syne">Auld Lang Syne</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Hotaru no Hikari&quot; (&quot;Glow of a firefly&quot;) is a Japanese song that sets new and entirely different words by Chikai Inagaki to the tune of the Scottish folk song &quot;<b>Auld Lang Syne</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hotaru-no-hikari">Hotaru no Hikari</a></li></ul></section>
